Patch: hotPath
Letter: H
Requires: none
Files: src/impl/formatter.js, src/impl/util.js, src/datetime.js, src/duration.js, src/impl/english.js, src/zones/systemZone.js
Summary: ten fast paths: six along the route a numeric token takes, four constants that were being rebuilt per call

H: avoidable work at ten points, in two families

Last, and grouped, because this is the tidy-up: one idea applied ten times, each
taking work out of a step without changing what the step means. None of them is
worth a reviewer's time alone — the smallest is worth nothing measurable on
either engine — while together they are most of what is left once the Intl calls
(A, B, E, F) are dealt with.

The first six are the route every numeric token walks, in order:

  formatter.js  num()        a formatter whose numbering system is latn can pad
                             digits itself; PolyNumberFormatter's Intl branch is
                             only needed by the ones that cannot.
  formatter.js  parseFormat  splitting a format string into tokens is pure, and
                             the same few strings recur, so the split is cached
                             rather than redone per value.
  formatter.js  token loop   the loop rebuilt its own state per token; hoisting
                             what does not vary keeps the switch monomorphic.
  util.js       roundTo      every rounding mode is the identity on an integer
                             once digits is non-negative, and an integer is what
                             every numeric token arrives as.
  util.js       padStart     month, day, hour, minute and second all pad to two
                             digits, so a "00".."99" table covers the common
                             case without building either string.
  datetime.js   tsToObj      integer civil math in place of a wrapper Date and
                             seven getter calls.

Two of those six are engine-specific, and they are the weakest part of this
patch: the token loop pays off on V8 and gives a little back on JSC, and the
padStart table is the reverse. They are in because the other four carry the
change, and splitting by engine would mean filing six patches to land four.

C subsumes two of them by construction — parseFormat and the punctuation half of
the token loop — so a tree that already has C gets less from that half of this
patch than the list above suggests.

The other four are constants that were being built inside the function that
reads them, and are simply moved to module scope:

  datetime.js   normalizeUnit       a 24-key table, allocated to read one key
                                    and dropped, on every startOf, endOf, set,
                                    get, hasSame and ISO precision.
  duration.js   normalizeUnit       the same again, 18 keys, on every Duration
                                    built from an object — so on every plus,
                                    minus, diff, shiftTo, as and get.
  english.js    formatRelativeTime  an 8-key table and the nine arrays inside
                                    it, plus a three-element list, per call to
                                    toRelative or toRelativeCalendar.
  systemZone.js offset              one Date per offset lookup, replaced by one
                                    Date reused. JS is single threaded and
                                    getTimezoneOffset reads it on the next line,
                                    so the instance cannot be observed in
                                    between.

None of the four captures anything, so each is a move rather than a rewrite, and
no output changes: 82,016 comparisons across five zones, nine units, the unit
aliases and the throwing path are identical on both engines.

They are worth listing separately from the six because they reach somewhere the
rest of the ladder does not. A, B, E and F pay for themselves only when a zone is
named; C, D and the six above only when something is formatted or parsed. These
four land on arithmetic (startOf -3% node / -27% bun, plus -7% / -19%, hasSame
-8% / -22%), on relative time (toRelativeCalendar -9% / -22%), and on
SystemZone, which is the default zone and therefore the configuration none of
the tables in benchmarks/upstream.ts name.

The engine split runs the same way as it does for the six: V8 escape-analyzes
some of these allocations away and JSC does not, so node sees -2% to -11% where
bun sees -6% to -27%. A fifth candidate of the same shape, the table of closures
impl/diff.js builds per call in highOrderDiffs, is deliberately not here: it
measured as nothing on either engine, both apparently inlining it, and a change
that cannot be measured is not worth a reviewer reading it.

